61 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "44066267-1187-5ad1-ae24-12c19959375d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571804 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571804 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571804 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98ea171d-32ec-53f1-bbbd-33159ec58a82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571841 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571841 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571841 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af071dd2-7bf2-5139-91db-22d623177322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571888 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571888 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571888 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c376bd76-aedb-536d-88ca-18c1489c93b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186571659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572063 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b089493b-c4d4-51a9-971f-914da25cc8a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572167 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186572063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572167 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572167 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572167 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=6x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨2,\\,12⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n\nII. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572167 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0a0a680-c34b-55b6-a46f-cafc346112a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572167 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(2,12)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=6x$ since $12=6\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,\\,12⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572211 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572211 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1acebc51-ffe4-5f71-9738-b7991922c9ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572211 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "589eeb11-b0b7-55ed-ad3a-22e0a871e976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572244 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572244 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "757d3965-2bf7-5ecb-8cc7-3977b2a1468d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572244 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572252 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572252 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b33180f-7eae-599e-bc